{
  "id": "u05-l01",
  "unit": "u05-numbers-time",
  "title": "One to ten",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "**এক** (*ek*) you already know. Here are the rest:\n\n**১ এক · ২ দুই · ৩ তিন · ৪ চার · ৫ পাঁচ · ৬ ছয় · ৭ সাত · ৮ আট · ৯ নয় · ১০ দশ**\n\nNumbers go **before** the noun. With people or family you can just say **আমার দুই ভাই আছে** 'I have two brothers'. For things, Bengali usually adds the classifier **-টা**: **দুইটা আম** 'two mangoes' — and the noun stays singular! (Kolkata says **দুটো**.)\n\nCareful: **ছয়** (6) rhymes with **নয়** (9), and **সাত** (7) and **আট** (8) are easy to swap.\n\n**নয়** (*noy*) 'nine' is spelled exactly like **নয়** 'is not'. Context tells you which: **নয় আর এক দশ** is 'nine and one is ten'.\n\nArithmetic is easy to say: **পাঁচ আর পাঁচ দশ** 'five and five (is) ten'.",
  "vocab": [
    {
